<BaseLayout title="Traffic measurement privacy — u64.cam" description="What u64.cam records for its transparent crawler and agent traffic experiment.">
  <article>
    <h1>Traffic measurement privacy</h1>
    <p>
      u64.cam runs a transparent experiment to understand how people, crawlers,
      feed readers, and AI agents discover public pages. Page requests pass
      through Cloudflare; selected request metadata is written to a private
      Cloudflare Analytics Engine dataset for up to three months.
    </p>

    <h2>Recorded</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Path without its query string, HTTP method, response status, and broad route category.</li>
      <li>Truncated User-Agent, Accept header, primary accepted language, and request purpose header.</li>
      <li>Referrer origin only; referrer paths and query strings are discarded.</li>
      <li>Country, Cloudflare data center, ASN and network organization, HTTP protocol, and TLS version.</li>
      <li>Cloudflare verified-bot, signed-agent, and bot-score signals when the account plan provides them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Not recorded by this experiment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Raw IP addresses, cookies, URL query strings, or stable visitor identifiers.</li>
      <li>Guestbook form bodies, names, notes, or other submitted fields.</li>
      <li>City, postal code, latitude/longitude, TLS fingerprints, or browser storage.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The fields are used to compare discovery channels such as normal pages,
      <a href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a>, <a href="/sitemap-index.xml">sitemaps</a>,
      <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>, canary pages, and the
      <a href="/guestbook/">guestbook</a>. User-Agent and network metadata are
      hints, not proof of a scraper's identity, and may be spoofed or proxied.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

